   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to giving notice of the hazard of early childhood tooth decay upon the sale of baby-bottles and sippy cups",
    "basePrintNo": "S8014",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8014-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §391-q, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8014\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the general business law, in relation to giving notice\nof the hazard of early childhood tooth decay upon\nthe sale of baby-bottles and sippy cups\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo require that all sippy cups include a warning regarding the risk of\nearly childhood tooth decay, or carries, caused by the prolonged use\nof these products.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 adds new section 391-q to the general business law providing\na definition for \"sippy cup\" and \"packaging\"; provides warning\nmessage; and specifies a civil penalty for violation.\n\nSection 2 provides for an effective date of one hundred eighty days\nafter it shall have become law.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nIt is crucial that parents and other caregivers are made aware of the\nhealth hazards to infant and children's teeth directly attributed to\nthe overuse of sippy cups. Sippy cups provide a convenience and a\nmeans of pacifying; parents and caregivers often allow a child to\ncarry the sippy cup for prolonged amounts of time, including time\nspent sleeping. During that time, sugary beverages other than water\ncan pool in the child's mouth causing tooth decay.\n\nAccording to the American Academy of Pediatric Dentistry, sippy cups\nare intended to wean a child off of a bottle and transition them into\nusing a cup. Parents often see sippy cups as a convenience, a\npacifier, and a way to reduce spilling in the home. The Academy adds\nfurther light to a Centers for Disease Control (CDC) report comparing\nthe dental health of Americans in 1988-1994 and 1999-2002. That\nreport found that while cavities decreased among older children,\ncavities in two- to five-year-olds actually increased 15.2 percent.\n\nThis legislation is intended to promote better oral health outcomes\namong infants and children.\nAn unintended, but positive, consequence of this legislation is the\nchance of a lower rate of\ndental malformations and other oral defects caused by the frequent use\nof duck-bill lidded vessels by small children.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2001-2002: A.10508\n2003-2004: A.5363\n2005-2006: A.4012\n2007-2008: A.4598\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\n\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become law.\n\n",
